  "rationale": "The spec demands 32 Msps throughput and <=2^-12 max error, minimising LUTs+FFs. Among my two allowed families, unrolled_k is the throughput-capable option: k chained micro-rotations per cycle gives one result every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles, so k=4 with N=14 yields 7-cycle latency and ~70 Msps at 500 MHz, comfortably clearing 32 Msps. iterative is the area-minimal option but its N+3 cycle latency (~17-21 cycles) puts it near the 32 Msps boundary, so it may or may not pass the throughput constraint. I split the budget 55/45: unrolled_k gets more because it is the likely feasible-throughput family and has an extra parameter (k) creating a richer trade-off surface (k vs Fmax vs area). iterative gets 45% to map the low-area corner and confirm its throughput feasibility. Both families share the same accuracy parameters (N, W, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding), so accuracy_bits will be comparable at matched N/W. I fix data_width=16 because the error constraint (2^-12) with N>=14 iterations is met at W=16 with margin, and wider W only adds area without improving the binding constraint. angle_guard is kept at -1..0 to minimise the atan-LUT area while N>=14 ensures convergence. frac_guard 0..2 explores a small area/accuracy trade-off. The p99 batch constraint (0.44 us) is easily met by both families at any plausible Fmax since even iterative's ~21 cycles at 400 MHz is only ~52 ns, far below 440 ns."
